            content: "A **hormone** is a chemical messenger: made in one place, released into your bloodstream, and read as an instruction somewhere else. Think of each one as a text message your body sends to itself — and your cells have **receptors**, specialized locks that only certain hormone keys can open.\n\nWhat makes hormones astonishing is their potency:\n• They work at **nanogram** (billionth of a gram) and even **picogram** (trillionth of a gram) concentrations\n• Free T4 thyroid hormone circulates at roughly **1–2 nanograms per deciliter** of blood\n• Estradiol is measured in **picograms per milliliter**\n• A change too small for any bathroom scale to detect can shift your weight, mood, and energy for months\n\nBecause doses are this tiny, hormone systems are exquisitely sensitive — small disruptions produce big symptoms, and small corrections can produce big improvements.\n\nOne more key idea: a hormone only acts on cells that carry its receptor. That's why one messenger can tell fat cells to store, muscle cells to grow, and brain cells to change your mood — same key, different doors.",
