            type: "example",
            title: "'It's Just Aging' — The Most Expensive Misdiagnosis",
            content: "Meet three people who were told the same thing:\n\n**Maria, 47**: exhausted for two years, gaining weight despite eating less, freezing in a warm office, hair thinning. Told: 'You're in your late 40s, this is normal.' Reality: **Hashimoto's hypothyroidism** — the most common cause of underactive thyroid in the US, affecting women 5–8 times more often than men. A daily microgram-dose tablet transformed her within months.\n\n**James, 52**: low drive, shrinking muscle despite gym time, flat mood. Told: 'Welcome to your 50s.' Reality: clinically **low testosterone** confirmed by two morning blood tests — a diagnosable, treatable condition, not a personality change.\n\n**Dana, 44**: suddenly terrible sleep, night sweats, anxiety, brain fog. Told: 'Probably just stress.' Reality: **perimenopause** — hormone swings that typically run 4 to 10 years before menopause, with well-studied treatment options.\n\nThe pattern: gradual, whole-body, nothing-hurts symptoms got misfiled as character or calendar. None of these people needed more willpower. They needed the right question asked — and a blood test that costs less than a dinner out. Aging is real, but 'just aging' should be a conclusion you reach AFTER checking the messengers, not instead of checking.",
