            title: "One Bagel, Two Very Different Bodies",
            content: "Same breakfast, two metabolisms:\n\n**Alex (insulin-sensitive)** eats a bagel. Blood glucose rises from 85 to about 125 mg/dL. The pancreas releases a modest pulse of insulin. Muscles — still stocked with yesterday's workout demand — soak up glucose eagerly. Within two hours, glucose is back near 90, insulin back near baseline. Total drama: none.\n\n**Sam (insulin-resistant, doesn't know it)** eats the identical bagel. Glucose climbs to 145. Sam's muscle and liver cells respond sluggishly to insulin's knock, so the pancreas **shouts** — releasing 3–5 times more insulin to force the same result. Glucose eventually settles, so a routine glucose test looks fine. But insulin stays elevated for hours, locking fat cells in storage mode. Around 3 p.m., glucose dips sharply from the oversized insulin dose and Sam feels shaky, foggy, and hungry for a snack — feeding the next spike.\n\nHere's the kicker: a standard checkup measures Sam's **glucose** (normal!) but almost never Sam's **insulin** (screaming). That's how the alarm gets missed for a decade. The test that would catch it — **fasting insulin** — costs a few dollars and is rarely ordered.",
