            content: "**Story 1 — Acute cortisol, doing its job.** Priya has a make-or-break presentation at 9 a.m. Her cortisol surges: glucose floods her bloodstream, her heart rate climbs, her focus narrows to laser precision. She delivers the best 20 minutes of her career. By lunch, cortisol has receded, she's relaxed and hungry, and that night she sleeps deeply. Spike, recovery, growth. This is stress as designed — decades of research show short-term stress can even **enhance** immune readiness and memory formation.\n\n**Story 2 — Chronic cortisol, wearing the body down.** Marcus has had 18 months of layoff rumors, a toxic manager, and 11 p.m. email checks. His cortisol never fully recedes. The result, in his own words: 'I'm tired all day but wired at midnight.' His waistline grows even though his weight barely moves — fat is migrating to his belly. His fasting glucose creeps up. He catches every cold going around the office. Same hormone as Priya's. Opposite outcome.\n\nThe difference isn't the stressor's size — it's the **shape of the curve**. Priya's cortisol was a wave: up, then fully down. Marcus's is a plateau: never high enough to feel heroic, never low enough to repair. Chronic elevation, not stress itself, is what breaks the system.",
