            title: "T4, T3, and the Conversion Most People Never Hear About",
            content: "Your thyroid mostly produces **T4 (thyroxine)** — but here's the twist: T4 is largely a **storage form**. It's like unmilled grain in a silo. The biologically active hormone, the one that actually docks into your cells and turns up the metabolic furnace, is **T3 (triiodothyronine)**.\n\nYour body converts T4 into T3 by clipping off one iodine atom, and most of that conversion happens **outside** the thyroid — in your liver, kidneys, gut, and muscles.\n\nWhy does this matter to you?\n\n• **The thyroid can be fine while conversion falters** — stress, severe calorie restriction, illness, and some nutrient deficiencies can slow T4→T3 conversion\n• **T3 is what your cells feel** — low T3 at the tissue level can mean low-thyroid symptoms even when T4 looks acceptable\n• **This is why testing only one number can miss the story** — a theme we'll return to\n\nThink of T4 as the paycheck and T3 as cash in hand. A good salary means nothing if the ATM won't dispense.",
