            content: "Follow the cascade through a single day.\n\n**Monday night**: You stay up past 1 a.m. finishing a show, alarm rings at 5:45. Roughly 4.5 hours of sleep.\n\n**Tuesday, 8 a.m.**: Ghrelin is running hot; leptin is muted. Your usual eggs feel insufficient. You add a pastry. \"Just today.\"\n\n**Tuesday, 3 p.m.**: The afternoon crash hits harder than usual. Sleep-deprived brains show heightened reward-center response to junk food — the vending machine's candy row is practically glowing. You get the M&Ms and chips.\n\n**Tuesday, 9:30 p.m.**: Dinner was normal-sized, but you're rummaging in the kitchen anyway. Fullness signals are muted 18%. You eat cereal standing up and feel like a failure.\n\n**Total damage**: research on sleep restriction finds people eat roughly **300-500 extra calories** on days after short sleep — mostly from snacks and carbs, exactly what the Spiegel/Van Cauter hormone shifts predict.\n\nNow multiply Tuesday by a chronically under-slept culture, and you can see why this isn't about weak people. It's about a hormonal environment quietly tilted toward eating — one that most people never learn they're standing in.",
