            type: "concept",
            title: "Progesterone: The Calming Counterweight",
            content: "If estrogen is the builder, **progesterone** is the stabilizer — and it deserves far more respect than \"the pregnancy hormone.\"\n\nMade primarily by the **corpus luteum** (the temporary gland left behind after ovulation), progesterone:\n\n• **Balances estrogen in the uterus** — estrogen builds the uterine lining; progesterone matures and stabilizes it. Estrogen without enough progesterone (\"unopposed estrogen\") drives heavy bleeding and, long-term, raises endometrial cancer risk — this balance is a core principle of gynecology\n• **Calms the brain** — its metabolite **allopregnanolone** acts on GABA receptors, the same calming system targeted by anti-anxiety drugs. This is why the high-progesterone luteal phase makes some feel serene and sleepy, and why its sharp premenstrual **withdrawal** contributes to PMS symptoms in sensitive brains\n• **Raises body temperature** — about 0.3-0.5°C after ovulation, the basis of temperature-based cycle tracking\n• **Supports sleep** — many women sleep differently across the cycle as progesterone rises and falls\n\nCrucial insight: **progesterone only appears in meaningful amounts after ovulation**. No ovulation = almost no progesterone. That single fact explains why conditions that block ovulation — like PCOS, coming up — throw the entire hormonal duet out of tune, not just fertility.",
