            type: "example",
            title: "Maria, 46: 'I Thought I Was Losing My Mind'",
            content: "Maria is 46, a project manager, and still has periods — mostly regular, occasionally strange. Over 18 months, her life quietly unravels:\n\n• She starts **waking at 3 a.m.**, wide awake, heart pounding\n• In meetings, she **loses words** mid-sentence — words she's used for 20 years\n• Her **anxiety spikes** for no reason she can name\n• Her knees and fingers **ache** in the morning\n• She's gained 8 pounds — **all at her waist** — without changing anything\n\nHer doctor runs a thyroid panel: normal. Offers an antidepressant. Suggests stress management. Nobody says the word *perimenopause* — because her periods haven't stopped.\n\nThis is the classic story. Maria isn't losing her mind; she's riding an estrogen rollercoaster with no map. **Sleep, mood, cognition, joints, and fat distribution are all estrogen-sensitive systems.** One connected explanation — but only if someone thinks to look.\n\nWhen Maria finally sees a menopause-informed clinician, the pattern is obvious in minutes. That's why knowing this material matters: the average woman will spend years in this transition. The informed ones navigate it; the uninformed ones just suffer through it.",
