            type: "concept",
            title: "The 11 P.M. Paycheck: Growth Hormone",
            content: "Melatonin opens the night shift — but the most valuable worker clocks in shortly after you fall asleep: **growth hormone (GH)**.\n\nGH is released in pulses, and the **single largest pulse of the entire 24-hour day** arrives during **slow-wave sleep (deep sleep)** — which is heavily concentrated in the **first third of the night**. In young adults, the majority of daily GH output can ride on that first deep-sleep window.\n\nWhat that pulse pays for:\n• **Tissue repair** — muscle recovery from training, wound healing, collagen synthesis\n• **Bone remodeling** — GH drives the bone-building side of the equation\n• **Metabolic housekeeping** — it shifts the body toward burning fat while sparing protein overnight\n\nNow the practical consequence: **the pulse is tied to sleep itself, not to clock time — but your deep-sleep pressure is greatest early in your night.** Chop the front of your sleep (late scrolling) or fragment it (alcohol — which specifically suppresses both slow-wave sleep quality and the GH pulse), and you shrink the biggest repair payment of the day.\n\nThis is why 'consistent bedtime' isn't just wellness fluff. An erratic schedule keeps hitting your deepest-sleep window at the wrong biological moment — shortchanging the exact hours when your body pays for its repairs.",
