            content: "Walk the book backward and watch the pattern snap into place:\n\n• **Insulin** — muscle handles **~80%** of insulin-stimulated glucose disposal. Strength training literally builds your glucose sink. (Levers 1, 4, 5)\n\n• **Cortisol** — the rhythm, not the hormone, is the problem. Morning light, breaks, breathwork, and people you trust restore the spike-and-recover shape. (Levers 2, 3)\n\n• **Thyroid** — needs a clinician when broken, but severe dieting suppresses T4→T3 conversion. Fuel it; don't starve it. (Levers 4, 5)\n\n• **Leptin & ghrelin** — Spiegel and Van Cauter 2004: two nights of 4-hour sleep pushed **ghrelin +28%, leptin −18%, hunger +24%**. Sleep IS appetite regulation. (Levers 2, 5)\n\n• **Estrogen & menopause** — strength training and body composition defend bone and metabolic health exactly when estrogen's protection fades. (Levers 1, 4)\n\n• **Testosterone** — Leproult 2011: one week of 5-hour nights cut levels **10-15%**. Lifting, sleeping, and losing visceral fat are the evidence-backed raisers. (Levers 1, 2, 4)\n\n• **Melatonin** — morning light and dim evenings move it more reliably than any supplement. (Lever 2)\n\n• **The mood trio** — walking, connection, and gut-friendly food (BMJ 2024; Holt-Lunstad 2010). (Levers 1, 3, 5)\n\nDifferent hormones. Same five inputs. **That was the book's real thesis all along.**",
